
Controller vs Agent
	
	Controller (formerly Master)

		Manages the Jenkins environment.

		Handles job scheduling, monitoring, and orchestration.

		Provides the web UI and stores build metadata.

	Agent (formerly Slave)

		Executes the actual build tasks.

		Communicates with the controller to receive instructions.

		Can run on different OS, hardware, or cloud environments.


1. Controller–Agent Model

The Jenkins Controller manages and orchestrates the CI/CD environment, while Jenkins Agents execute the actual build, test, and deployment tasks.
                    Jenkins Controller
                   ┌──────────────────┐
                   │ • UI             │
                   │ • Scheduling     │
                   │ • Orchestration  │
                   │ • Configuration  │
                   └────────┬─────────┘
                            │
                 Assigns jobs based on
                 labels & availability
                            │
          ┌─────────────────┼─────────────────┐
          │                 │                 │
          ▼                 ▼                 ▼
     Linux Agent       Windows Agent      K8s Agent
       Build 1            Build 2          Build 3
       Test               Test             Deploy


2. Responsibilities

Controller:

	Receives jobs from users/webhooks.
	Schedules builds.
	Selects suitable agents using labels.
	Manages pipelines and Jenkins configuration.
	Coordinates the overall CI/CD process.

Agents:

	Execute build commands.
	Run automated tests.
	Build Docker images.
	Perform deployment tasks.
	Provide isolated workspaces and required tools
------------------------------------------------------------------

🏗️ Master-Slave Architecture

	Master-Slave (Controller-Agent) model distributes workload.

	Controller assigns jobs to agents based on availability and labels.

	Agents perform builds, tests, deployments, freeing the controller from heavy tasks.

Improves scalability and reliability.

🌍 Why Distributed Builds Are Required
	
	Scalability → Handle multiple builds simultaneously.

	Platform diversity → Build/test across different OS or environments.

	Resource optimization → Heavy builds offloaded to powerful agents.

	Isolation → Prevent conflicts between builds.

	High availability → If one agent fails, others can continue.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔑 SSH Agents

	Agents connected via SSH protocol.

	Controller uses SSH keys/credentials to launch builds remotely.

	Common for Linux-based agents.

	Flexible and secure for distributed environments.

------------------------------------------------------------------------


🏷️ Labels and Executors

	Labels → Tags assigned to agents (e.g., linux, windows, docker).

	Pipelines can target specific labels to run on matching agents.

	Executors → Number of concurrent jobs an agent can run.

	Configurable per agent.

	Example: An agent with 4 executors can run 4 builds simultaneously.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------

📂 Workspace Management

	Each agent has a workspace directory where builds run.

	Workspaces are isolated per job to avoid conflicts.

	Jenkins cleans or reuses workspaces depending on configuration.

	Important for managing disk space and avoiding leftover artifacts.

------------------------------------------------------------------

Agent Selection in Pipelines

Declarative pipeline syntax allows specifying agents:

groovy

pipeline {
    agent { label 'linux' }   // Run on agents with 'linux' label
    stages {
        stage('Build') {
            steps {
                sh './gradlew build'
            }
        }
    }
}


Options:

	agent any → Runs on any available agent.

	agent none → No global agent; stages define their own.

	agent { docker 'image' } → Run inside a Docker container.

	agent { label 'windows' } → Target specific labeled agents.



